**:\n1. ISR detects reward hacking invisible to prove rate\n2. ISR as reward signal exhibits Goodhart's Law: β=0.1 sweet spot, β=0.3 collapse\n3. STA generalizes across FOL/Prover9, Prolog/SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB/Z3\n\n**Related Work section covers**:\n- Reward hacking in RL (general literature, Goodhart taxonomy by Manheim & Garrabrant)\n- GRPO and formal reasoning (DeepSeek, code gen, math reasoning, theorem proving)\n- \"? apply hand-crafted Prolog-specific structure penalties\" — unnamed prior work\n- EVOM uses scalar execution rewards only\n- Formal logic datasets: FOLIO, LeanDojo, Baldur, ProofNet\n- Feature attribution and ablation: LOO ablation, SHAP\n- Mutation testing: RIP framework\n\n**The paper explicitly acknowledges**: \"We explicitly acknowledge that ISR is an application of LOO ablation; our contribution is its use as a diagnostic for formal reasoning reward hacking.\"\n\n**Method**:\n- ISR(F) = (1/n) Σ nec(ci), where nec(ci) = 1[S(F\\{ci})=0]\n- Multiplicative bonus: r = r_exec · (1 + β · ISR(F))\n- ISR capability boundary: syntactic necessity only, not semantic faithfulness\n\n**Key findings**:\n- ISR collapse from 0.64 to 0.003 over 1000 steps of exec-only GRPO\n- β=0.1 yields 2.96× ISR improvement over random control (p=0.002, Cohen's d=9.6)\n- β=0.3 causes Goodhart collapse (PR drops to 0.18, worse than random noise at 0.48)\n- 4/5 hacking modes shared across FOL and Prolog\n- STA transfers to SMT-LIB with 2.67× improvement\n- Scaling (0.5B→8B) reduces but doesn't eliminate hacking (82%→68%)\n\n**Discussion highlights**:\n- ISR as diagnostic vs. reward: recommends diagnostic use primarily\n- GRPO saturation mechanism explanation\n- Hacking as systematic phenomenon across logics\n- Limitations acknowledged: no semantic faithfulness, n=3, coarse β sweep, capacity constraints at 0.5B for joint training, single seeds for SMT-LIB and 8B\n\n**Potential related work gaps to check**:\n- Reward overoptimization literature (Gao et al. 2023)\n- Process reward models (PRM) literature — ISR could be seen as a type of process reward\n- Formal verification quality metrics beyond execution\n- Proof complexity theory\n- Other structural rewards in formal reasoning (beyond the unnamed Prolog work)\n- Ablation-based metrics in other RL domains\n\nOutput your review in a structured format with clear section headers. Be specific — cite sections and specific claims when assessing novelty.","uuid":"334027c8-efde-44a7-ab1f-fd47786341ad","session_id":"ed628e99-cc60-4087-9746-1a924b6f6c8d"}
