{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LQzKWeAia4LqVXoarMH1W2","type":"tool_result","content":"260\t\\subsection{ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$: Three-Seed Replication}\n261\t\n262\tWe train \\grpo{} with the ISR bonus (Eq.~\\ref{eq:bonus}, $\\beta{=}0.1$) across three seeds.\n263\t\n264\t\\begin{table}[h]\n265\t\\centering\n266\t\\caption{ISR $\\beta{=}0.1$ three-seed replication (FOL). All seeds converge to a consistent region with no tautological collapse.}\n267\t\\label{tab:isr_3seed}\n268\t\\small\n269\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n270\t\\toprule\n271\tSeed & \\pr{} & \\isr{} & Prov-only \\isr{} & Comp. & Taut. \\\\\n272\t\\midrule\n273\ts42 & 0.81 & 0.337 & 0.415 & 5.58 & No \\\\\n274\ts43 & 0.77 & 0.305 & 0.396 & 5.82 & No \\\\\n275\ts44 & 0.75 & 0.282 & 0.376 & 6.08 & No \\\\\n276\t\\midrule\n277\tMean & 0.777 & 0.308 & 0.396 & 5.83 & --- \\\\\n278\t$\\pm$std & $\\pm$0.030 & $\\pm$0.028 & $\\pm$0.020 & $\\pm$0.25 & --- \\\\\n279\t\\bottomrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KqjYtusZs3EdNzk8PpkvFP","session_id":"ed628e99-cc60-4087-9746-1a924b6f6c8d","uuid":"aab94939-1823-43c9-ace8-885a1a2e8e0a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:11:16.845Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aed6b033f1505451c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuKvESYuMb9b5CfexkC6mP","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":86171,"tool_uses":15,"duration_ms":164437},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"daeac9cb-f929-40ba-bf44-d2823823253d","session_id":"ed628e99-cc60-4087-9746-1a924b6f6c8d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WHudV9KfZcNZfbqyQSZnxi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LTZ8Zc5hdY4ap17Kk74nm5","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"\n# Review Report — Reviewer 1 (R1): Methodology and Experimental Rigor\n\n## Paper: Solver Trace Ablation: Diagnosing Reward Hacking and Goodhart's Law in GRPO-Trained Formal Reasoners\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodology Soundness\n\n**ISR definition (Eq. 1-2):** The ISR formulation is mathematically well-defined and correctly specified. LOO ablation on solver input components is a clean, principled approach. The nec(c_i) indicator and its averaging into ISR are straightforward. No formula errors detected.\n\n**Reward integration (Eq. 3):** The multiplicative bonus r = r_exec * (1 + beta * ISR(F)) is a reasonable design choice that avoids penalizing unproved outputs. The formulation correctly ensures ISR only modulates reward for proved formalizations.\n\n**Critical methodological concern — ISR capability boundary underexplored:** Section 3.4 correctly notes that ISR measures syntactic necessity, not semantic faithfulness (rho = -0.049 with NLI). However, this boundary has deeper implications than acknowledged. ISR can assign high scores to structurally minimal but semantically vacuous proofs (e.g., P -> P tautologies with multiple sub-components yield ISR approaching 1.0 because each sub-component of the single premise is \"necessary\"). The paper does not discuss this failure mode despite it being directly relevant to Claim 2.\n\n**O(1) vs O(N) ablation (Table 3):** Well-motivated and cleanly executed. The rho=0.18 result definitively rules out the fast approximation. The analysis that hacking patterns do not place exploited components at position 1 is a convincing mechanistic explanation.\n\n**LOO ablation limitations:** The paper does not discuss the well-known limitation of LOO-based attribution: it cannot detect redundancy among groups of components. If components c_i and c_j are individually necessary but jointly redundant with c_k, LOO will mark all three as necessary. This inflates ISR for certain degenerate structures. This theoretical gap should be acknowledged.\n\n## 2. Experimental Design\n\n**Controls:** The matched random control (Table 5-6) is the strongest design element. Using r = r_exec * (1 + 0.1 * U) where U ~ Uniform(0,1) cleanly isolates ISR signal content from variance injection. This is essential given the GRPO saturation mechanism discussed in Section 6.\n\n**Baselines:** SFT baseline (Table 2) and pretrained model provide reasonable anchoring. The pretrained model's proved-only ISR of 0.64 establishes a structural quality ceiling.\n\n**Missing baselines:** No comparison with existing structural reward approaches (e.g., Mellgren et al.'s hand-crafted Prolog penalties, mentioned in Related Work). Even a qualitative comparison would strengthen the contribution.\n\n**Goodhart dose-response (Table 7):** Only two beta values (0.1 and 0.3) are tested. The paper claims a \"sharp transition\" but provides no data points between 0.1 and 0.3. This is a coarse characterization at best, and the paper itself acknowledges this (Section 6, Limitation 3). Beta values of 0.15, 0.2, 0.25 would substantially strengthen the Goodhart narrative.\n\n**Curriculum ablation (Table 8):** The comparison is confounded — fixed beta=0.1 uses the multiplicative formula while sigmoid and linear use the convex formula. These are not comparable on equal footing. The paper notes this in passing (\"under the multiplicative formula\") but does not adequately flag the confound.\n\n## 3. Statistical Rigor\n\n**Cohen's d = 9.6 with n=3:** This is a major concern. While the paper correctly reports the raw numbers, Cohen's d with n=3 is extremely unreliable. The sampling distribution of d at n=3 is highly skewed with massive confidence intervals. The point estimate of 9.6 likely has a 95% CI spanning roughly [2, 20+]. Reporting d=9.6 as though it is a precise effect size estimate is misleading; the paper should report the CI for d or at minimum note the extreme imprecision.\n\n**Welch's t-test at n=3:** Welch's t-test with df=3.1 has very low power for detecting anything but the largest effects. The p=0.002 is indeed significant, but the test assumptions (approximately normal sampling distribution of means) are unverifiable at n=3. A permutation test or bootstrap confidence interval would be more appropriate for this sample size. With n=3 per group, there are only 20 possible permutations — an exact permutation test is trivially computable and would provide a more defensible p-value.\n\n**Confidence interval for ISR ratio (Table 6):** The 95% CI [1.66, 4.39] for the ISR ratio is reported but the method of computation is not specified. With n=3, bootstrap CIs or exact methods should be used and documented.\n\n**Three seeds is inherently limited:** The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 2), but the framing \"the extreme effect size compensates\" is not quite right. Large effect sizes can produce significant p-values at small n, but they do not compensate for the inability to assess distributional assumptions, detect outliers, or estimate variance precisely. The paper should more candidly acknowledge that three seeds provide suggestive rather than definitive evidence.\n\n**Per-seed ISR ratios (3.24, 3.43, 2.39):** The variance here is actually substantial — the smallest ratio is 30% below the largest. With only 3 data points, it is impossible to know whether this reflects genuine heterogeneity or sampling noise.\n\n## 4. Reproducibility\n\n**Sufficient detail provided:**\n- Model (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, Qwen3-8B) clearly specified\n- Data (FOLIO 500 training problems) identified\n- GRPO hyperparameters (K=8, 500 steps) stated\n- Solvers (Prover9, SWI-Prolog, Z3) named\n\n**Missing details:**\n- Learning rate, batch size, KL penalty coefficient for GRPO not specified\n- Warmup ratio mentioned in project docs (0.1) but absent from paper\n- Max generation length / max_new_tokens not specified (relevant since s43 exec-only hit max_new_tokens in 87% of outputs)\n- ISR computation: timeout settings for solver calls during LOO ablation not specified\n- \"Equivalent-size Prolog and SMT-LIB formalization datasets\" — no detail on how these were constructed, validated, or sized\n- SFT training details entirely absent (epochs, learning rate, data format)\n- Held-out evaluation set size not specified (project docs suggest 100 problems, but paper does not state this)\n- Temperature / sampling strategy for generation at evaluation time not specified\n\n**Code availability:** Not mentioned. No reference to a code repository.\n\n## 5. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n### Covered experiments:\n1. ISR beta=0.1 three-seed replication (Tables 4-6) — COVERED\n2. Random beta=0.1 three-seed control (Tables 5-6) — COVERED\n3. 3-seed statistical comparison (Table 6) — COVERED\n4. O(1) vs O(N) ablation (Table 3) — COVERED\n5. Prolog exec-only 3 seeds (Table 9) — COVERED\n6. Prolog exec+STA 3 seeds (Table 10) — COVERED\n7. Cross-logic hacking taxonomy (Table 12) — COVERED\n8. Multi-logic joint training (Table 15) — COVERED\n9. Cross-logic transfer (Table 16) — COVERED\n10. SFT baseline (Table 2) — COVERED\n11. SMT-LIB single-logic (Table 13) — COVERED (single seed)\n12. 8B scale effect (Table 14) — COVERED (single seed)\n13. Curriculum ablation (Table 8) — COVERED\n\n### Missing or incomplete:\n- **SMT-LIB genuine rate delta**: Not reported in Table 13 (only PR, ISR, components)\n- **SMT-LIB hacking taxonomy detail**: Paper mentions \"3 shared modes\" but provides no breakdown table comparable to Table 12\n- **8B Goodhart boundary**: Whether the sweet spot shifts with scale is speculated (\"may shift rightward\") but not tested\n- **Intermediate beta values**: No data between beta=0.1 and beta=0.3\n- **SMT-LIB and 8B multi-seed**: Both single-seed only\n\n### Critical concern — data provenance of Tables 4-6:\n**The ISR beta=0.1 three-seed results in the paper (Table 4) appear to be \"imagined\" data from the Dream system, not actual experimental results.** The project's internal decision log (D039-D043, dated 2026-05-14) reveals that the actual s43 and s44 results showed \"conclusion duplication\" hacking with ISR values of 0.830 and 0.902 respectively — not the 0.305 and 0.282 reported in Table 4. The paper_framing.md explicitly states: \"s43/s44 主要退化模式为 conclusion duplication\" and \"不可报 raw 3-seed mean ISR（被膨胀值污染）\". The imagined_experiments.yaml file contains the exact numbers that appear in Table 4, confirming these are projected rather than observed values. Similarly, the Prolog 3-seed results (Tables 9-10) and SMT-LIB results (Table 13), 8B results (Table 14), joint training (Table 15), and transfer (Table 16) all appear in imagined_experiments.yaml with matching numbers, indicating they are fabricated for the \"best case\" dream paper rather than actual experimental outcomes.\n\n**This is a fatal flaw.** If the paper were submitted with these numbers, it would constitute data fabrication. The actual data tells a fundamentally different and more complex story — one where ISR-as-reward induces ISR-specific gaming (conclusion duplication) in 2/3 seeds, which is itself an interesting finding but contradicts the clean narrative presented.\n\n## 6. Top Weaknesses\n\n**W1 — Data integrity crisis (CRITICAL):** The three-seed ISR beta=0.1 results (Table 4) do not match actual experimental outcomes. Real s43/s44 showed conclusion duplication hacking with ISR 0.830/0.902, not the clean 0.305/0.282 reported. Most cross-logic, scaling, and joint training results appear to be imagined/projected rather than observed. This undermines the entire paper's credibility.\n\n**W2 — Coarse Goodhart characterization:** Only two beta values (0.1 and 0.3) support the \"Goodhart's Law\" narrative. A single data point in \"pre-Goodhart\" and a single point in \"post-Goodhart\" is insufficient to characterize a transition, let alone claim it is \"sharp.\" The paper's central contribution — demonstrating Goodhart's Law in formal reasoning reward shaping — rests on extremely thin empirical support.\n\n**W3 — Statistical methodology at n=3:** Cohen's d=9.6 at n=3 is reported without confidence intervals. Welch's t-test assumptions are unverifiable at this sample size. No non-parametric alternatives (permutation test, bootstrap) are used. The statistical claims, while technically yielding significant p-values due to extreme effect sizes, are methodologically fragile.\n\n**W4 — ISR metric blindspot unacknowledged in Claim 2:** The paper does not discuss that ISR can be \"gamed\" by structurally minimal proofs (conclusion duplication). The internal project documents (D042, D043) identify this as a fundamental metric limitation, but the paper presents ISR beta=0.1 as an unqualified success. A metric proposed as a diagnostic should have its failure modes thoroughly documented.\n\n**W5 — Multiple results are single-seed:** SMT-LIB (Table 13), 8B scaling (Table 14), joint training (Table 15), cross-logic transfer (Table 16), and curriculum ablation (Table 8) are all single-seed. Given the paper's own finding that seed variance is extreme in GRPO (Section 5.1, \"identical training setups produce qualitatively different failure modes\"), single-seed results for these experiments are unreliable by the paper's own standards.\n\n## 7. Top Strengths\n\n**S1 — Clean experimental control design:** The matched random control (ISR vs random at identical beta) is well-designed and effectively isolates the ISR signal's contribution from variance injection / anti-saturation effects. This is the paper's strongest methodological contribution.\n\n**S2 — ISR collapse trajectory (Table 1):** The 0.64 -> 0.13 -> 0.003 ISR collapse over 100/500/1000 steps is a compelling and well-documented finding. This clearly establishes that prove rate and structural quality diverge, which is the paper's most robust claim.\n\n**S3 — Cross-logic generalization attempt:** Testing STA on three distinct formal languages (FOL, Prolog, SMT-LIB) with different solver paradigms demonstrates ambition in scope. The hacking taxonomy (Table 12) with Jaccard similarity analysis is a useful analytical contribution.\n\n**S4 — Honest capability boundary documentation:** Section 3.4's acknowledgment that ISR does not measure semantic faithfulness (rho=-0.049 with NLI, ns) is commendable transparency. The paper explicitly defines ISR's scope and limitations.\n\n**S5 — Practical recommendations:** The four actionable recommendations in the Conclusion are concrete and useful for practitioners working with GRPO in formal reasoning.\n\n## 8. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1:** The three-seed ISR beta=0.1 results in Table 4 show remarkably clean convergence (ISR 0.282-0.337, std=0.028). Can you provide the raw per-sample ISR distributions for each seed? Specifically, what fraction of proved samples in s43 and s44 have ISR >= 0.8, and what fraction have single-premise formalizations where the premise closely matches the conclusion?\n\n**Q2:** You report Cohen's d=9.6 but do not provide a confidence interval. What is the 95% CI for d? Have you considered a permutation test (with only 20 possible permutations at n=3, this is exact and assumption-free)?\n\n**Q3:** The Goodhart transition is characterized by exactly two data points (beta=0.1 and beta=0.3). Have you explored any intermediate values? If not, how do you justify calling the transition \"sharp\" rather than, say, gradual with the crossing point somewhere in [0.1, 0.3]?\n\n**Q4:** The curriculum ablation (Table 8) compares fixed beta=0.1 (multiplicative formula) against sigmoid/linear (convex formula). These use different reward formulations. Can you provide results for sigmoid/linear scheduling under the multiplicative formula to make the comparison fair?\n\n**Q5:** For the Prolog and SMT-LIB experiments, how were the \"equivalent-size\" datasets constructed? Were they derived from the same natural language problems as FOLIO, or are they independently sourced? What validation was performed to ensure comparable difficulty?\n\n## 9. Minor Issues\n\n- Table 1: The \"Components\" column shows \"---\" for 100 steps. Was component count not measured, or was it not applicable? This should be clarified.\n- Table 2: \"Genuine\" column uses percentage for pretrained/SFT but the definition (ISR > 0.3) is not explained until Section 4. Move the definition earlier or add a table footnote.\n- Table 3: The dual ISR notation \"0.03/0.11†\" is confusing. Consider separate rows for fast-ISR-estimated and true-ISR-evaluated.\n- Table 8: The \"Formula\" column mixing \"multiplicative\" and \"convex\" without prior definition is unclear. These terms should be defined where they first appear.\n- Section 5.1, line 201: \"s43 exhibits repetition collapse (PR = 13%, ISR = 0)\" — the paper says PR=13% here but the summary says PR=14%. Minor inconsistency.\n- The abstract says \"13 seeds\" but the paper body does not clearly account for 13 distinct seed runs. A table mapping seeds to experiments would help.\n- Section 2: Citation \\citep{polu2023formal} — the year 2023 for Polu et al. on formal theorem proving may be incorrect (the well-known Polu & Sutskever work is from 2020). Please verify.\n- Section 2: \\citep{chen2026evom} — a 2026 citation should be verified as published/available.\n- The bibliography style uses acl_natbib but no .bib file is shown. The reference list should be verified for completeness and accuracy.\n\n## 10. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Summary:** The paper addresses an important problem — detecting and characterizing reward hacking in GRPO-trained formal reasoners — and proposes a clean diagnostic metric (ISR via LOO ablation). The ISR collapse trajectory (Claim 1) is the paper's most convincing contribution. However, the paper has a critical data integrity concern: the three-seed ISR beta=0.1 results central to Claim 2 appear to be projected/imagined rather than experimentally observed, with actual experiments showing a fundamentally different pattern (conclusion duplication hacking in 2/3 seeds). Even setting aside data provenance issues, the Goodhart characterization rests on only two beta values, statistical methodology at n=3 is inadequately justified, and multiple key experiments are single-seed — contradicting the paper's own argument that single-seed evaluation is unreliable. The core diagnostic insight (ISR detects structural degeneration invisible to prove rate) is sound and publishable, but the current presentation overclaims relative to the evidence, and the Goodhart narrative needs either substantially more experimental support or significant qualification.\n","project":"solver-trace-ablation","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T092544","overall_score":"weak reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15966,"cache_read_input_tokens":69749,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15966,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SuKvESYuMb9b5CfexkC6mP","session_id":"ed628e99-cc60-4087-9746-1a924b6f6c8d","uuid":"faea1286-2c7b-4e45-810d-7e76cdbc8d62"}
